Differentiable rendering has gained significant attention in image-based inverse problems due to its potential to enhance traditional optimization-based methods and offer self-supervision for learning-based approaches, particularly those lacking ground truth-annotated training data. However, existing differentiable renderers are either limited by their failure to account for light source visibility, resulting in inaccurately rendered shadows, or hindered by slow computation, preventing them from efficiently training deep models. To address these issues, we introduce an accurate yet high-performance approach that leverages spherical harmonics approximations to model scene illumination and visibility, using spherical approximations for occluding surfaces to significantly speed up shadow computation compared to ray tracing-based methods. By formulating this approach as a differentiable system, we can efficiently utilize analysis-by-synthesis optimization to recover texture, illumination, rigid pose, and geometric deformations from images. Moreover, by combining this approach with a neural network, we can efficiently solve image synthesis and single-view depth regression problems, while also estimating camera intrinsic parameters. We evaluate the effectiveness of our approach on several benchmark datasets and compare it to existing methods. Our code and 3D reconstructions are publicly available. This work has been submitted to IJCV. Next, we introduce ICRV, the first conference solely focused on implicit computer vision methods. ICRV includes a new paper collection with a significant number of recently published key papers in the field, along with two prominent keynote speakers. Additionally, ICRV features a workshop dedicated to the development of next-generation implicit modeling techniques inspired by biology. Furthermore, a separate workshop is organized on novel optimization methods for computer vision, along with a special session on qualitative reasoning in computer vision, incorporating four invited presentations. Lastly, ICRV includes a new symposium comprising an invited presentation and a panel discussion on the potential implications of computer vision advancements on society. We hope to see you in Tokyo at ICRV! 2022. \\ \begin{figure}[h] \centering \includegraphics{icrv_logo.png} \includegraphics{icrv_slogan.png} \end{figure} 

